    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d235">

                <pb facs="0222" n="172" sameAs="frus:frus1918Russiav03/pg172"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">File No. 600.939/32</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">The Secretary of State to
                        the Minister in China</hi> (<hi
                            rendition="#italic">Reinsch</hi>)</head>
                <opener>
                    <seg rendition="#center">[Telegram]</seg>
                </opener>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Washington</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1918-02-02T15:00:00-05:00"><hi rendition="#italic">February 2,
                                1918, 3 p.m.</hi></date></dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>Your January 28, 5 p.m. The American Government can not assent to the prohibition
                    of the export to Siberia of merchandise owned by Americans. You will so
                    represent to the Foreign Office and say that in the opinion of the American
                    Government any embargo on exports to Siberia, particularly at this time, is
                    inadvisable. <pb facs="0223" n="173" xml:id="pg173"/> The attitude of this
                    Government towards the Bolsheviki is set forth in the President’s recent address
                    to Congress<anchor n="1" xml:id="d235-f1"/> and in Department’s telegrams of
                    January 16, 4 p.m.,<anchor n="2" xml:id="d235-f2"/> and January 21, 7
                        p.m.<anchor n="3" xml:id="d235-f3"/></p>
                <closer>
                    <signed><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                            >Lansing</hi></signed>
                </closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
    <standOff>
        <listAnnotation>
            <note corresp="#d235-f1" n="1" xml:id="d235-f1-content">
                <p>The President’s address to Congress of Jan. 8 stated war aims and attitude toward
                    Russia.</p>
            </note>
